1: FABLE_CRIME_P1_20260913 — Fable 5.1 (claude-fable-5-1)
2: 
3: (Verbatim answer of the flagship adjudicating seat on the `crimewidthkill` pass-1 design review, fired LAST on 2026-09-13
4: as an Agent-tool subagent pinned to `model: fable`, READ-ONLY, after the seven scriptable seats
5: (`panel_20260913_000135/`) and the Opus measuring seat (`opus_pass1_20260913/`).  Brief:
6: `_scratchpad/brief_crime_fable_p1_20260913.md`.  The harness rendered `&`, `<` and `>` as HTML entities; restored here,
7: nothing else changed.)
8: 
9: ---
10: 
11: FABLE_CRIME_P1_20260913
12: claude-fable-5-1
13: `# CHECKPOINT -- 2026-09-13 ~00:3x   HEAD 9c91999 (records)   crimewidthkill PASS 1 IN FLIGHT`
14: 
15: **VERDICT: MEMBER 3 -- DECLINE + LATCHED `(once)` ANNOUNCE, placed AT the width arm, `goto` to the existing common tail (the `#498` `sgi_de` shape), on a 4-cell floor {1,2}x{R,W} plus the accept cells and one latch-count row; FAULT is rejected because its only warrant is a header comment its own proposer concedes does not establish rejection, and two of its four seats argued from a premise the tree refutes.**
16: 
17: Evidence basis: all nine seats (7 scriptable + Opus report + this seat; agy deliberately not fired per the 2026-08-30 ruling, which is neither a failure nor agreement). Everything below marked MEASURED is from the Opus artifacts I opened, not from the brief's summary of them; everything marked READ I opened in `GXEMUL-SEC/`.
18: 
19: ---
20: 
21: ## Q1 -- Member 3 or 5: count the arguments
22: 
23: **The 4-4 seat split is a 2-vs-5 argument split.** Two of the four FAULT seats rest on a false premise about the fix space:
24: 
25: - **minimax**: "Members 1-4, 6, 7 run the switch. Member 5: no -- return 0 exits before the switch." **deepseek**: "FAULT is the smallest non-terminating change that avoids executing the switch side effects." Both are arguments *against fall-through*, not *for a bus error*. The tree already contains a decline that skips the switch -- `dev_sgi_re.c:1214-1219` `goto de_declined`, label at `:1438` (READ) -- and Opus MEASURED it: `rows_decline.out` shows `sh CTL+08 0x800 halted=no`, `sb CTL+08 0x01 ctlmsg=0`, `revhack lb pc=0xbfc05184`. Packet-fed seats could not see `#498`; that is a brief limitation, not a seat failure, but the argument does not survive contact with the tree. Remove those two and FAULT has codex and kimi-k3.
26: 
27: **The arguments that remain for 5, ruled on individually:**
28: 
29: 1. **`crimereg.h:39` "all registers 64-bit access"** (codex). READ. It is a comment on `CRIME_BASE` in NetBSD's *driver* header -- it describes the discipline the driver follows, not what the ASIC does on a narrower cycle. And it is not even true of the guests: the file's own TODO at `dev_sgi_ip32.c:327-328` records NetBSD 1.6.2 writing 4 bytes at `0x00c`, and the model serves `len == 4` today. codex's own sentence: "does not establish the hardware's rejection behavior." **Ruling: caution about implementing lanes (against member 7); zero warrant for a bus error.**
30: 2. **"A decline answers 0 from the memory/interrupt controller, which also asserts unestablished behaviour"** (codex, kimi-k3's asymmetry: a byte read of INTSTAT returning "nothing pending" is a lie). True that both dispositions assert something no document supports. But the asymmetry cuts both ways -- FAULT's guest-visible consequence for that same access is a DBE, which for a kernel interrupt path is a panic, not a wrong value -- and it is unevidenced in exactly the way the premise-audit rule warns about: it is a *plausible* story about a device class, not a fact about CRIME. Meanwhile the 8- and 4-byte reads of INTSTAT/INTMASK remain correct, so a narrow-read 0 corrupts no modelled state. **Ruling: symmetric and unevidenced; it cannot overturn a house policy adjudicated twice on this machine with a measured precedent.**
31: 3. **"The IP32 guest ecosystem already absorbs DBE (ten `dev_unreadable` slots)"** (kimi-k3, inherited from the brief). READ and REFUTED by codex, confirmed by me: the ten `device_add("unreadable ...")` sites are at `machine_sgi.c:241-247` (IP20) and `:345-351` (IP22/24), each after an unconditional `fatal("TODO..."); abort();` at `:219-220`, `:267-268`, `:275-276`. **IP32 installs none.** Nothing in this tree's IP32 model depends on DBE delivery. The premise was mine in the pass-1 brief and it was false as a live statement.
32: 4. **"FAULT uses the architectural failure path."** It uses *an* architectural path. Whether real CRIME raises a bus error, ignores the cycle, or serves a lane is not established (premise 6 holds: `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` has no CRIME document; the author's "On my real O2" note at `:193-198` is about mirrors and silent on width).
33: 
34: **The arguments for 3:**
35: 
36: 1. **The `#498` precedent transfers, and the objection to transfer is aimed at the wrong thing.** The shape is identical -- width guard, accept set {4,8}, side-effecting switch, common tail -- and the *warrant* recorded at `dev_sgi_re.c:440-447` and `:1183-1212` is device-independent: "A guest choosing an access width is not an emulator error" and "`return 0` would ... raise EXCEPTION_DBE -- turning a refusal into a guest bus error." "RE is graphics, CRIME is the memory controller" is an argument about the *cost of a wrong disposition*, and that cost is unevidenced in both directions (point 2 above). What transfers is the warrant; what would block transfer is a CRIME-specific fact, and there is none.
37: 2. **Observability under free running.** MEASURED (`rows_fault.out` freerun row: `txt_after_continue ... failed=0`): FAULT's only host-side trace is `memory_rw.c:604`'s `debug()`, suppressed when free-running. The decline prints its `(once)` line in the same cell. The house rule is not merely "a guest-drivable diagnostic needs a latch"; its premise is that a refused guest action *announces itself once and survives `-q`* so that a real guest doing this is discoverable. Under pure FAULT a real guest that ever issued a narrow CRIME access would show as an unexplained guest panic with nothing on the host. kimi-k3's "5 + latched announce" repairs this -- but then 5+latch and 3 differ only on DBE-vs-0, which is point 2 again.
38: 3. **Guest liveness is the fork's objective.** Decline keeps the guest running (`pc+4`, no exception); FAULT vectors it. With no guest known to issue these accesses (READING, all file-reading seats), the difference matters only for a buggy or hostile guest, and the fork's disposition for that case has been decline-once on this machine six times over (`#497`'s six guards, `#498`).
39: 4. **`return 0` already has a meaning in this tree: "nothing is here"** (`dev_unreadable.c`). CRIME is present and answering at two widths. Borrowing the absent-device signal for a present-device width refusal is a wrong statement about the machine (kimi CLI, Opus, grok all made this point independently).
40: 
41: **Members 1, 2, 4, 6, 7**: 1 is MEASURED to halt the machine (`rows_delete.out` `sh CTL+08 0x800 halted=yes`) and to fire the PROM PC hack (`revhack lb pc=0x80004000`); 2 is 3 without the instrument; 4 is incoherent (the arms compute nothing for len 1/2 -- codex, kimi, grok); 6 misdiagnoses (storage exists); 7 needs a lane rule for 64-bit bitfield registers with no guest needing it and no warrant -- the addition licence refuses it, and grok's point stands: the `(once)` line is what would *earn* member 7 a warrant later.
42: 
43: **Eighth members**: both proposals (5+latch; IMPLEMENT-reads/DECLINE-writes) are compositions, not new dispositions. Named, not adopted.
44: 
45: ---
46: 
47: ## Q2 -- Placement: at the width arm with `goto` (Opus), not the early guard (codex)
48: 
49: What a refused access executes under the Opus diff (`diffs/decline.diff`, READ against `dev_sgi_ip32.c`): `:207` HARDINT snapshot; `:210-211` `idata` decode (writes only; no side effect); `:223-229` REV/CONTROL/INTSTAT refresh; `:238-254` RAM-size checks; `:256-267` bank loop; the else arm at `:290` -- latch test, `fatal` once, `goto crime_common_tail`; the tail `:384-390` -- HARDINT restore (no-op, nothing changed it), INTSTAT recompute (idempotent), `memory_writemax64(..., 0)` on reads, `return 1`.
50: 
51: **Does executing the prelude on a refused access matter? No, and it is measured rather than reasoned:** the prelude is a pure function of `use_fb`, SOFTINT, HARDINT, `physical_ram_in_mb` and `reg[BANK0]`, so a refused access leaves exactly the state any accepted access leaves. `acc 8/4 r+w` is byte-identical across all nine binaries, and `sb +0x37`/`sh +0x36` after an accepted `sw` read back the unchanged word in every build. The only thing the prelude *does* on a refused access that codex's hoist would avoid is the `-M` divisibility kill -- which fires on the first CRIME access *of any width*, so hoisting merely changes which access trips a launch-config defect. That is a behaviour change unrelated to this round, in a round that should be minimal.
52: 
53: **Three reasons against the hoist:** (i) `goto` forward past `int mb_per_bank; int flag_for_128MB; size_t total_mb;` is legal C99 (no VLA) but is exactly the kind of thing a reviewer stops on; (ii) codex's hoist was argued for FAULT, where skipping the tail is harmless because nothing needs delivering -- under DECLINE the tail must be on the path, and `#498`'s comment records why a private `return 1` "diverges the day the tail grows"; (iii) the guard-in-place is the smaller diff and the shape already adjudicated at `:1214-1219`.
54: 
55: **Latch shape:** Opus's `int warned_len` works (`malloc`+`memset(0)` at `:406-407` zero-inits it). I direct a **`warned` bitfield with a `CRIME_W_LEN` bit** instead -- same cost, and side finding (b) below will want `CRIME_W_CTL` without a second struct edit. Separate bits per site, never shared (`#498`'s `mC` 23/25).
56: 
57: ---
58: 
59: ## Q3 -- The floor, by the vacuity rules
60: 
61: Clauses (a)-(c) are carried by the committed witness: 3/15 pre-fix (`witness_committed.out`), 15/15 on the fix, kills a verbatim `exit(1)` reinstatement, and a comment-only file still carries the kill. The (d) DEPENDS-ON enumeration over the edited else arm: dominating predicates `len == 8`, `len == 4` -> operand `len`, reachable partition **{1, 2}** (MIPS GPR widths are 1/2/4/8; `lwl/lwr/ldl/ldr` issue the aligned full width; `0x280` is 8-aligned so no end-of-device clamp -- Opus/codex READING, I concur); the reached body (tail) tests `writeflag` -> partition **{R, W}**. Floor cells: **{1,2} x {R,W} = 4**, plus the repair's own effect.
62: 
63: | rows | class | mutant it kills | channel (MEASURED) |
64: |---|---|---|---|
65: | **Switch side-effect rows, one per cell**: 2W `sh 0x0800 -> +0x08` (not halted, RAM trip prints); 1W `sb 0x01 -> +0x08` (no CONTROL text); 1R/2R `lb`/`lh` at +0 from pc `0xbfc05180` (pc not redirected) + the live control `revhack lw ctl` | **FLOOR (d)** | `fallsw` (goto deleted = repair nulled, guard kept), `len1only`, `readonly` | `halted=yes` / `ctlmsg=1` / `pc=0x80004000` in `rows_fallsw.out`, `rows_len1only.out`, `rows_readonly.out`; each escapes the 15-row witness |
66: | **Disposition rows**: reads `v0 == 0`, `pc == pc+4`, `exccode == 0` (sentinel-seeded); writes `pc+4`, `exccode 0` | **FLOOR (d)** -- `goto -> return 0` keeps the guard and reroutes the repair | `return 0` shape (and any lane-serving shape: `v0` would read `0x11` at REV+7) | `rows_fault.out`: `v0=0xdeadbeef pc=...80000180 exccode=7`. NOTE: the write-side *readback unchanged* row is a NON-VARYING channel (identical in fault/decline/delete) -- disclose it, do not count it as a kill |
67: | **Latch count**: 5 refused accesses in one session -> exactly one `(once)` | **FLOOR (L3)** -- sole carrier of "latched" | `nolatch` | `compare.out`: nolatch differs from decline *only* on `latch x5` (decl=5) |
68: | **Accept cells**: 8R, 8W, 4R hi, 4R lo, 4W hi, 4W lo | **FLOOR (de-escalation clause)** -- the fix deletes a host-fatal width guard, so its accept side is the fix's cost | growth mutants `len == 4 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE`, `len == 4 && !(relative_addr & 4)`, `len == 8 && writeflag == MEM_READ`, ... | `acc 8/4 r+w` covers five of six; **4W-hi (a `sw` at +0 of a scratch slot) is missing and is owed**, and each accept row owes one MEASURED kill under its growth form (accept-row failability) -- not yet built |
69: | Per-width own-session announce rows carrying `len=N` | contract / CLAIM->ROW (second channel) | `len1only` on the len=2 cell (already killed above) | keep; cheap; not floor |
70: | `M48 lw +0x04` ramkill | control that the RAM check is untouched | -- | identical in all builds |
71: 
72: **Disclosed under (e), with warrants:** `return1` is EQUIVALENT (`compare.out`: 0 differing lines) because `memory_rw.c:491-492` (`#95`) pre-zeroes the read buffer -- **an absence record with a time-indexed warrant: any edit to that `memset` re-runs this death measurement**; INTSTAT/INTMASK reassert and TIME cells (idempotent / host clock -- non-varying); the REV *write* direction of the PC hack (READING); widths 3/5/6/7 (unreachable via the guest path, derivation above -- codex's "direct callback" test for them would be rung 1 and is not owed).
73: 
74: The `PRETABLE.out` obligation applies (kill-table-backed detector); its CHANNEL line is already answerable at writing time for every row above, which is the check the mandate asked the first application to make.
75: 
76: ---
77: 
78: ## Q4 -- Side findings: file, severities, none owed as code
79: 
80: - **(a) CONTROL lane/label** -- restated, because the brief's sentence would send a round to the wrong line: the 4-byte arm is internally consistent (BE halves). The defect is the **switch**: `case CRIME_CONTROL` matches only the exact label `0x08` and tests raw `idata`, so a NetBSD-style 32-bit write at `0x00c` -- where bit 11 of a 64-bit BE register actually lives -- never reaches the reset/watchdog logic (`rows_*: sw CTL+0c 0x800 halted=no`, all builds). Guest-visible functional gap (a guest reboot through CRIME_CONTROL at `0x00c` does nothing). **MEDIUM, file as `crimectllane`.** Records obligation *this round*: the detector contract must say that the `sh CTL+08` row's channel exists **because** of this defect; a lane fix moves it.
81: - **(b) `[ CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented control ]` unlatched** -- guest-drivable `fatal()` per write, cannot be silenced by `-q`: the flood class. **MEDIUM, file under `floodclass`.** Not this round (different class from a kill; the one-site scope rule), but the bitfield latch shape above is the zero-cost preparation.
82: - **(c) `-M 48` lazy kill** -- host-set value, guest-set timing. The `> 1024` check in the handler is dead (`machine_sgi.c:479-481` rejects it at construction, READ); the divisibility check is live. **LOW**; disposition is member 7 at `dev_crime_init` with a rung-2 construction witness.
83: - **(d) Bank interleave** -- READ: the loop writes contiguous `0x208..0x240`; `crimereg.h:141-148` names CTRL1 `0x218`, CTRL2 `0x210`, CTRL3 `0x228`, ... With `-M 64` a guest reading per the header sees bank 2 populated and bank 1 a duplicate of bank 0. Whether NetBSD/OpenBSD memory sizing survives that (they may sum populated banks regardless of index, or use ARCBIOS descriptors) is **not established -- needs a boot witness before ranking**. File as `crimebankorder`, provisionally LOW-MEDIUM.
84: 
85: ---
86: 
87: ## Q5 -- Which of your statements is wrong
88: 
89: 1. **"the CRIME-RE width guards (#497/#499/#513)"** -- only `#497` is the width guards; `#499` de-fatalised the unmatched-address catch-all and `#513` the six read arms (`dev_sgi_re.c:452-488`). Wrong record; correct it in the round block.
90: 2. **"The panel split 4-4"** -- by seat. By argument it is 2 vs 5: minimax and deepseek argued that only FAULT skips the switch, which the tree and the measurements refute (Q1). Do not carry the 4-4 into the record without that qualifier.
91: 3. **Side finding (a)'s wording** -- "the 4-byte arm puts bit 11 on the high half" points at the arm; the defect is the switch's exact label plus raw `idata` (Q4). A round editing the arm from that sentence would break the accepted 4-byte convention.
92: 4. **"`sb 0x01 -> +0x08` | DECLINE: silent"** -- `rows_decline.out` shows `decl=1` on that row: it announces `(once)` (first in session). "Silent" should read "no CONTROL text".
93: 5. **"`machine_sgi.c` installs it ten times" was your pass-1 premise and codex's correction is right but understated**: not merely "not on IP32" -- none of the ten is live on *any* machine in this fork, because each sits after an `abort()`.
94: 6. **"`return1` is EQUIVALENT ... (memory_rw pre-zeroes)"** -- correct and verified, but it is an absence record and must name its warrant and expiry (`memory_rw.c:491-492`, `#95`); as written it is a bare equivalence claim.
95: 7. Everything else in the measured table I checked against the row logs holds: FAULT sentinel kept / `0x80000180` / ExcCode 7; five `failed` lines vs one `(once)` vs five default-arm lines; free-running FAULT prints nothing; `delete` halts on `sh 0x0800 -> +0x08` and redirects the PC.
96: 
97: **Provenance and limits.** I built nothing and ran nothing; every MEASURED cell above is Opus's artifact opened by me (`opus_pass1_20260913/out/rows_*.out`, `scripts/compare.out`, `diffs/*.diff`, `witness_committed.out`), each ending in its sentinel. The one thing this ruling still lacks that a seat could supply is the accept-row failability measurement (six accept cells, growth mutants) -- it is owed before the detector ships, not before this design is adopted.
