5. **`bugs529.md:11–14`**

   > “So a 32-bit `sw 0x800` to `+0x08` -- the HIGH half of the 64-bit register on this big-endian machine -- carries bit 11 into `CRIME_CONTROL_HARD_RESET` and stops every CPU, while a 32-bit write to `+0x0c`, the lane that holds bit 11 and the offset NetBSD 1.6.2 writes (the arm's own TODO says so), falls to the default arm and does nothing.”

   **Contradiction:** “Does nothing” is too broad. The accepted-width code performs the register store, and default emits a debug diagnostic. The preserved observation expressly reports `dflt=1`, `halted=no`: [rows_base.out:20](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/opus_pass1_20260913/out/rows_base.out:20). See also [device source:282](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c:282) and `:403–407`.

   **Correction:** “A 32-bit `sw 0x800` at `+0x08` incorrectly stops every CPU; the corresponding `+0x0c` write performs the register store and reaches default logging, bypassing CONTROL’s reset/watchdog handling.”

6. **`ledger_529.py:267–269`, generated `crimectllane` title**

   > “dev_sgi_ip32.c's CRIME_CONTROL arm matches the exact label 0x08 and acts on the raw idata of any width: a 32-bit sw 0x800 to +0x08 (the HIGH half on this big-endian machine) stops every CPU while NetBSD's +0x0c write does nothing -- #529's sw08/sh08 rows use it as a channel and owe re-derivation when it is fixed”

   **Contradiction:** The same preserved `+0x0c` observation and source cited in finding 5 show a store and default diagnostic.

   **Correction:** “CRIME_CONTROL acts on raw `idata` at exact label `0x08`: a 32-bit reset write there stops every CPU, while the `+0x0c` write bypasses reset/watchdog handling; #529’s dependent rows require re-derivation when the lane model is fixed.”

